From 14baf04b46e4bc4c239fdef3c57cc12f9fc1774c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Toly <1397674+AnatolyRugalev@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:03:38 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/45] blitz-dom: patch cost proportional to depth, not
 document size

Four changes to make an incremental resolve cost what the change touches:

- Construction skips elements whose subtree carries no damage (their
  layout children and layout_parent links are still valid), instead of
  walking the whole tree every resolve.
- The layout cache grows per node (LayoutCache, same matching rules as
  taffy's Cache). A content-sized container k levels deep is measured
  under about 3 + 5k distinct constraints per pass, so taffy's 9 entries
  evicted each other in a cycle and every unchanged sibling of the
  changed path missed, cascading down its subtree.
- Rounding is incremental: set_unrounded_layout records nodes whose
  layout changed (inline layout records its root), and only those, plus
  the subtrees of moved nodes, are rounded again. Debug builds check the
  result against taffy::round_layout.
- Speculative relayout boundaries: a changed flex or grid item is asked
  again every question its container asked it last time (the cache now
  keeps full inputs); when every answer matches, the container keeps its
  old cache instead of laying out all its items again.

With the writing-mode feature, which lays each box out in its own frame,
rounding stays the physicalising pass and nothing is speculated.

Measured (release, one changed cell per frame): in a tree of
groups of 8, resolve goes from 68.8 ms to 0.016 ms at 262,144 cells; in
a flat 50,000-cell flex grid, from 28 ms to 5.8 ms.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
